                            {
                                "target_word": "meromorphic",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "19eac020-fddf-3565-9abe-fc029032bec8",
                                        "entry_id": "22e93073-5545-352c-8093-2bcf6c4b1f09",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "That is the ratio of two holomorphic functions (and so possibly infinite at a discrete set of points)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "not-comparable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "10f01761-5227-4d61-8a8b-f315f8c1cefd",
                                                "sense_id": "19eac020-fddf-3565-9abe-fc029032bec8",
                                                "text": "Normal families of meromorphic functions are most naturally studied using the spherical metric (§1.2), an approach initiated by Ostrowski [1926]. Some results for meromorphic functions, such as the FNT, are immediate extensions from the analytic case, whereas others, such as Landau's or Julia's theorem are set in a much broader context than their analytic counterparts. Normality criteria pertinent to families of meromorphic functions, such as Marty's theorem, have not yet been encountered."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "00fd4acb-a539-4d26-aa50-b913dfa587c1",
                                                "sense_id": "19eac020-fddf-3565-9abe-fc029032bec8",
                                                "text": "Note that such a transformation is holomorphic at the origin, but is essentially singular at infinity. However, since T(z) commutes with A(z), the transformed system has coefficient matrix A(z)-zq'(z)I and hence is again meromorphic at infinity."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "47388524-8c4d-4234-aa93-8bb21e1f80d1",
                                                "sense_id": "19eac020-fddf-3565-9abe-fc029032bec8",
                                                "text": "A point p#92;inP¹ is singular for #92;textstyle#92;frac#123;d#125;#123;dz#125;-A if the equation cannot be made regular at p with a local meromorphic transformation."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
